WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.700 --> 00:00:04.759
Despite appearing to be nothing more than
a cute little kitty, this merciless creature

00:00:04.759 --> 00:00:10.030
is one of the most powerful entities in Captain
Marvel, and potentially the entire MCU.

00:00:10.030 --> 00:00:10.660
Don't believe us?

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:11.660
Ask Nick Fury.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:15.040
Here's everything you need to know about the
"cat" in Captain Marvel.

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:15.760
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:16.780 --> 00:00:21.140
In the comics, the idea of Carol having a
loyal pet kitty predates the occasion of her

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:25.410
officially taking the mantle of Captain Marvel,
with the cat making its first appearance in

00:00:25.410 --> 00:00:28.290
2006's Giant-Size Ms. Marvel #1.

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:32.369
But much like in the movie, in which it takes
up until the third act for the other, alien

00:00:32.369 --> 00:00:36.480
shoe to drop, the cat spent years in the comics
being seen as just a cat.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.070
It only reached its full, fearsome potential
as an alien "flerken" during the space-set

00:00:41.070 --> 00:00:42.980
2014 run of Captain Marvel.

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.579
In the comics, Carol first meets her cat in
an imagined alternate dimension conjured up

00:00:47.579 --> 00:00:52.060
by Scarlet Witch during the "House of M" storyline,
but is fortunate enough to see the creature

00:00:52.060 --> 00:00:56.210
cross back over into her reality with her
when that illusion comes to an end.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.430
In the movies, though, the cat is introduced
as a companion of Annette Bening's Mar-Vell,

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:02.400
another alien in disguise hiding out on Earth.

00:01:03.330 --> 00:01:07.620
Though Captain Marvel's cat has only appeared
in one movie so far, it's already responsible

00:01:07.639 --> 00:01:12.069
for one of the most consequential actions
in the entire Marvel Cinematic Universe scratching

00:01:12.069 --> 00:01:13.020
out Nick Fury's eye.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.180
Ever since he was introduced in the surprise
post-credits coda to 2008's Iron Man, Samuel

00:01:18.180 --> 00:01:23.660
L. Jackson's Nick Fury has worn a patch over
his left eye, implying a fairly badass backstory.

00:01:23.860 --> 00:01:27.100
In Captain America: The Winter Soldier, Fury
hinted at what happened …

00:01:27.100 --> 00:01:30.080
“Last time I trusted someone, I lost an
eye.”

00:01:30.689 --> 00:01:35.060
Now, more than 10 years after being introduced
in the movies, we finally know the origins

00:01:35.060 --> 00:01:38.009
of Nick Fury's signature injury the flerken
did it!

00:01:38.009 --> 00:01:42.270
While "I lost my eye to an alien" is a pretty
cool story to tell over drinks, it loses a

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:46.380
little bit of that cool factor when the alien
in question looks like a typical housecat.

00:01:46.780 --> 00:01:51.159
So Fury has been letting rumors take hold
and embracing the mythmaking around his lost

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:51.720
eye ever since.

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:57.200
In the Captain Marvel movie, Carol's cat is
pegged as a flerken by Talos the Skrull, who

00:01:57.200 --> 00:02:01.289
reacts to the creature's presence with what
turns out to be well-justified fear.

00:02:01.289 --> 00:02:05.300
But in the comics, the cat's true identity
was first noticed by another deceptively cute

00:02:05.300 --> 00:02:08.580
character Rocket Raccoon of Guardians of the
Galaxy fame.

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:14.000
When Rocket first meets Carol's cat, he immediately
sets out trying to engage it in combat, hoping

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.880
to kill it before it gets the chance to kill
him.

00:02:16.280 --> 00:02:20.680
Despite Carol's protests, Rocket raves about
the monster's fearsome powers, including a

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.120
carnivorous mouth full of hellish tentacles.

00:02:23.360 --> 00:02:27.540
Though at first he sounds paranoid about the
creature's true nature, he's eventually proven

00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:32.099
right when the flerken quietly lays dozens
of eggs, tipping Carol off to her fuzzy friend's

00:02:32.100 --> 00:02:33.420
curiously alien nature.

00:02:34.450 --> 00:02:39.440
Captain Marvel uses its '90s setting as an
excuse for an in-universe prequel throwback,

00:02:39.440 --> 00:02:44.060
centering the action on the MCU Phase One's
Tesseract, a.k.a. the magical blue cube that

00:02:44.060 --> 00:02:45.860
houses the blue Space Stone.

00:02:46.170 --> 00:02:50.580
In the movies, this consequential cube spent
time apparently being protected by the belly

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:55.300
of the flerken, sitting undigested in its
terrifying stomach until the choice was made

00:02:55.300 --> 00:02:57.049
to vomit it up on a desk at S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:57.049 --> 00:03:01.660
headquarters at some undisclosed later time,
as shown during Captain Marvel's second end-credits

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:02.160
scene.

00:03:02.560 --> 00:03:06.480
As the comics make clear, the belly of the
flerken isn't just unusually stretchy it's

00:03:06.580 --> 00:03:11.260
actually its own pocket dimension, capable
of containing unknown multitudes of matter,

00:03:11.300 --> 00:03:12.320
organic or otherwise.

00:03:12.420 --> 00:03:16.560
It's no wonder people are so scared of the
thing it doesn't just swallow people whole.

00:03:16.560 --> 00:03:20.760
It completely removes them from their own
reality, sending them off to worlds unknown.

00:03:21.500 --> 00:03:25.700
Despite being in a universe where it's been
firmly established that animals and plants!

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:30.670
can talk, the flerken in Captain Marvel generally
keeps its lips sealed, only opening its mouth

00:03:30.670 --> 00:03:35.640
to hilariously devour entire hallways' worth
of Kree soldiers, among other untold atrocities.

00:03:35.640 --> 00:03:39.920
As a result, there's not a lot of character
to this character, at least not that we get

00:03:39.920 --> 00:03:40.660
to see.

00:03:40.660 --> 00:03:41.800
What does the flerken want?

00:03:42.060 --> 00:03:46.720
In both the comics and the movies, the flerken
is generally used as a joke unto itself, created

00:03:46.720 --> 00:03:50.800
for what seems like the sole purpose of setting
up the punny line "No one steals my flerken

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:51.300
cat."

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:53.560
It also serves as a vehicle for cute pop culture
references.

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:58.020
In the comic book run in which its backstory
as a fearsome alien is introduced, the cat

00:03:58.180 --> 00:04:01.440
is known as Chewie, after the well-known Star
Wars character.

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:02.719
“Chewbacca?

00:04:02.720 --> 00:04:03.220
Okay, well, haha.

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:07.820
You’re gonna need a nickname because I ain’t
saying that every time.”

00:04:08.460 --> 00:04:12.040
This makes sense, considering that story takes
place while Carol is in space.

00:04:12.040 --> 00:04:16.120
The movie augments this, connecting the cat
to the Air Force base on which Carol and Mar-Vell

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:20.460
both work, and giving it the name of Goose
in reference to the '80s classic aerial combat

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:25.110
movie Top Gun — most definitely a favorite
film for movie ace pilot Carol...though her

00:04:25.110 --> 00:04:28.970
trip through Blockbuster video seems to indicate
she might want to give The Right Stuff a try

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.360
the next time she's back on Earth...

